    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d512">

                <pb facs="0598" n="503" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg503"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13356: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Canton</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Spiker</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Canton</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-01-29T16:00:00+08:00">January 29, 1936—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to my telegram of January 20, 4 p.m.,<anchor n="55" xml:id="d512-f1"/>
                    local military charge [<hi rendition="#italic">authorities?</hi>] have just
                    confirmed yesterday’s press reports that about 20,000 Communists under command
                    of Hsiao Ko<anchor n="56" xml:id="d512-f2"/> and Ho Lung<anchor n="57"
                        xml:id="d512-f3"/> moved southward from Wengan and after heavy fighting with
                        <pb facs="0599" n="504" xml:id="pg504"/> Government forces succeeded in
                    occupying Pingyueh and Maping-chang on the 26th, thus controlling main route
                    from east to Kweiyang toward which Central Government troops have retired.
                    Today’s press quotes Kweiyang reports that Kweiting has been taken and that
                    enemy is now attacking Lungli, 70 li east of Kweiyang, which is defended by only
                    two divisions. Yunnan authorities allegedly rushing aid to Kweiyang where
                    populace is terror stricken and assisting garrison in digging entrenchments east
                    of railway. Communist objective believed to be juncture with Communist forces in
                    Szechuan. Kwangsi forces reported to be remaining aloof from fighting and to be
                    holding defensive positions in southern Kweichow in areas described in my
                    telegram of January 15, 10 a.m.<anchor n="57a" xml:id="d512-f4"/></p>
                <p>Recent advices from American missionaries in Kweichow show no Americans in
                    Kweiyang and that two in Kiensi and one each in Pingpa, Anping and Anshun were,
                    in accordance with this Consulate General’s previous advices, very carefully
                    watching developments preparatory to evacuation if situation warrants. Further
                    telegraphic warning has been sent missionaries concerned. Department, Peiping,
                    Nanking, Hankow informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Spiker</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
